WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.980
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here — in the flesh, this time.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:10.400
You guys may or may not know this, but Wisecrack
Editions take a really long time to make.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.060
And we’ve been wanting to do something where
we can quickly react to what’s currently on TV or in theaters,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.040
so we figured we’d try it out with season 3 of Rick and Morty.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.620
And hey, if you like this, let us know.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:25.340
We’d love to do these on things like Game of Thrones, Stranger Things, whoever this Jake Paul person
is, you name it.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:30.920
In Rickmancing the Stone, we see Rick, Summer, and Morty traversing a Mad Max-inspired post-apocalypse.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.360
What struck me most about this episode was an
idea that has popped up in previous episodes,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.420
but hasn’t been as fleshed out as fan favorites like “existence is pain” and “everything you believe is bullshit."

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.580
That idea - modern alienation.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:53.220
Through a bright, glowing rock, a mustachioed
murderer, and Jerry,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.560
the episode explores another depressing-as-fuck idea:

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.440
that society may be slowly destroying our souls.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.540
Welcome to this Wisecrack Quick Take on Rickmancing
the Stone.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.520
And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.100
But first, a quick recap:

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:13.720
Rick, Morty, and Summer infiltrate the aptly named Death Stalkers to steal a glowing rock that Rick wants for science.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.860
Morty and Summer use this aggressive world to vent all their confused emotions about their parent’s divorce.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.580
Summer does all kinds of acting out, then falls for a swole dude with a bucket on his head

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:31.960
and eventually goes through her own divorce —
allowing her to contextualize her parents' separation.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.980
Morty vents his frustration by living vicariously
through a revenge-driven arm,

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.520
and eventually realizes he should just let it go.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.560
And Rick acts like a child, kind of.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.800
Early in the episode we hear Rick introduce
the idea of alienation,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.160
suggesting that Summer’s tension with Jerry has made her disillusioned,

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:12.560
and the only thing that can make her feel alive is to travel to a place where civilization has crumbled.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.320
Now the clever thing here is that the show not
only tackles alienation on a micro level —

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.060
the family kind that Summer and Morty experience —

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.680
but on a macro level: the alienation of society as a whole.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.760
And through this, the show frames how we lose
agency in our everyday lives.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.380
Case in point: Jerry.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.760
I think this quote informs a lot of what’s
going on in this episode :

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:48.420
What Rick is not-so-subtly suggesting is Jerry’s
complete and utter lack of personal resolve,

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.760
power, agency, whatever you want to call it.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.580
He’s a doormat to the world.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:58.420
This idea of powerlessness informs a lot of Morty’s resentment towards Jerry as evidenced by this...

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.440
and this.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.400
But Morty’s bitterness goes much further
than Jerry’s failed marriage.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.780
And that’s because, in some ways, Jerry
is the everyman of the modern world.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:35.120
Since the days of the industrial revolution, people have been complaining about loss of control in the modern world.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.060
Instead of having our own trade or farm, many
toil away at monotonous jobs.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.660
This loss of autonomy is reflected on by countless
authors, scholars, and films,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.420
but we might as well just throw this example at you:

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.000
And this is what frames Morty’s anger at
Jerry’s powerlessness.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.080
Jerry is the pinnacle of modern stoogery.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:20.220
His existence as a cog in a meaningless machine
is really driven home in the Rickshank Redemption,

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:26.340
where the Galactic Federation keeps everyone compliant and docile through a liberal use of pharmaceuticals.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.600
In other words, Jerry is so bad at being a person, he thrives in a society devoid of freedom.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:49.060
His inability to assert himself is put on
display in every interaction he has with Rick.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.820
Of course, there is a version of Jerry who
isn’t, in Rick’s words,

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.820
“an inert chunk of randomly assembled molecules drifting wherever the universe blows,”

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.160
and that would be Jerry from Cronenberg Earth.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:04.780
When stripped of the trappings of modern society, Jerry goes from being a useless hack in a loveless marriage,

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.340
to a total badass who seems to be keeping his marriage together.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.260
This, in itself, is hilarious.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.980
But with this latest episode, I think Rick
and Morty is playing with this idea

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:20.120
that the trappings of modern society are inherently
alienating and create Jerrys out of all of us.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:26.100
After Rick introduces electricity to the Wasteland,
it soon resembles a modern industrialized society.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.880
Summer’s violent lifestyle has been traded
in for everyday banalities.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.000
She isn’t out murdering mutants, she’s
grocery shopping for human bits.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.160
The savagery of the Death Stalkers has been
reduced to complaining about recycling.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.340
And Hemorrhage, the murdering machine, is now
a good-for-nothing couch potato.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:51.680
What’s so clever here is the implication that modern comforts are what drive our banal, terrible lives.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.900
With no mutants to fend off, people resort
to bickering about trivialities.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.500
Due to electricity, people aren’t murdering each other in the Blood Dome, they’re watching blood-dome.

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:12.800
See, most post-apocalyptic fiction functions as a cautionary tale of how awful our lives might become,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.840
but of, of course, being the darkest show on TV,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.720
Rick and Morty goes out of its way to prove that modern life is its own special brand of soul crushing.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.300
Our comforts eventually alienate us from meaning,
creating different kinds of misery.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.620
Or as Rick says about ruining Summer’s marriage:

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.400
So, here's an interesting question that's a bit speculative,

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:42.000
but does Summer forgive her father because she sees that modernization turns EVERY man into an actionless lump?

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.620
Even if you're a post-apocalyptic badass?

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.440
Now I want to speculate a little bit about Jerry here.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.980
It’s likely that Rick will stay the same
ol’ uncaring ass he’s ever been,

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.420
but I think we’re going to see a lot of character growth in his family members — specifically Jerry.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:00.000
What if he has a Tyler Durden moment with
the Cronenberg Jerry?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.940
What if he, like Edward Norton, goes from being a disaffected modern buffoon to a man of action?

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.980
I mean the show has countless references to popular cinema,

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:13.180
and I think a Fight Club shout-out would work perfect for Jerry’s season 3 arc.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:16.460
But hey — I guess we’ll see as season 3 continues.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:17.600
Thanks a lot for watching, guys.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.880
If you want to hear more about this week’s
Rick and Morty episode,

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:24.780
we actually just released the first episode of the Wisecrack podcast where we talk all about —

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:25.840
you guessed it —

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:26.820
Rick and Morty.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.700
So click the link in the description to check
out our podcast and let us know what you think.

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.660
We’ve got more Rick and Morty stuff coming
out, so make sure to subscribe.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:34.680
Squanch ya later.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:35.240
Peace.

